7. Business rules
1. One attendance record per employee per work date; a work date is the organization-local calendar date.
2. Timestamps are stored in UTC and displayed in the organization timezone (default Asia/Kolkata).
3. Leave balance changes only when a request is approved, and never goes below zero.
4. An employee cannot have two overlapping active leave requests.
5. Payroll amounts are computed on the server; clients never submit computed totals.
6. HR cannot modify ADMIN accounts.
7. HR records are deactivated, not hard-deleted.
8. AI output is advisory only and is never used to decide pay, promotion, discipline or termination.
8. Non-functional requirements
Area
Requirement
Security
bcrypt hashing; JWT access plus rotating refresh tokens; Helmet; CORS allowlist; rate limiting; Zod validation on every input; NoSQL-operator and mass-assignment protection; private signed file access; audit logging; no secrets in source or logs
Authorization
Backend authoritative for every request; IDOR tests for every resource
Performance
Paginated lists (max 100); indexed queries; target p95 API latency under 500 ms on seeded data under normal load; route-level code splitting
Reliability
Health and readiness endpoints; graceful Socket.IO reconnect; clear errors; no stack traces in production
Accessibility
Keyboard navigable, visible focus, AA contrast, meaningful empty/loading/error states
Responsiveness
Usable on desktop, tablet and mobile
Observability
Structured logs with request ids; log levels; no passwords or tokens logged
Maintainability
Modular monorepo; business logic in services; shared Zod schemas and types; OpenAPI generated from schemas
Testability
Unit, integration, authorization, component and E2E tests; CI runs lint, typecheck, test and build
Deployability
Environment-driven config; documented deployment to Vercel, Render, Atlas, Cloudinary and Gemini
9. Key user journeys
1. Onboard an employee: Admin logs in, creates a department, creates an employee with a temporary password; the employee logs in and is forced to change it.
2. Daily attendance: Employee checks in and out; duration and lateness appear on their history and in the HR view.
3. Leave lifecycle: Employee applies for leave; manager or HR is notified live, approves; employee receives a live notification and the balance updates.
4. Performance review: Manager or HR creates a review, generates an AI summary draft, edits and finalizes it; the employee sees the finalized review and is notified.
5. Policy question: Employee asks the assistant about leave rules and gets a referenced answer; an out-of-scope question gets a refusal that points to HR.
6. Reporting: HR filters a report by date and department and exports it; the export is audit-logged.
10. Release acceptance criteria
• Login, logout, refresh and protected routes work; unauthenticated requests return 401 and wrong-role requests return 403.
• All four roles are enforced on the backend; employees cannot access another employee's payroll, documents or reviews.
• Employee CRUD, attendance, leave lifecycle, payroll records, documents and reviews work end to end in the browser.
• Notifications persist and appear live, including after a reconnect.
• The AI assistant and summary work through the backend; output is schema-validated; prompt-injection tests do not bypass authorization; no API key appears in the web bundle.
• Reports export with correct role scoping.
• Lint, typecheck, all tests and the production build pass; no major console errors.
• README, architecture, security, deployment, test plan and runbook documents are accurate and known limitations are listed.
